The following lemma shows that vertex 3 fires the most in all parallel chip-firing games on KnK_{n} with n≥4n\geq 4.
Lemma 3.9.
For any n≥4,n\geq 4, all parallel chip-firing games on KnK_{n} satisfy f3≥fif_{3}\geq f_{i} for all 1≤i≤n1\leq i\leq n.
Proof.
Since deg+⁡v3=1\deg^{+}v_{3}=1, it must fire once in each period for every chip received in that period. Note that v3v_{3} receives chips from all other vertices except v4v_{4}. Thus, f3≥fif_{3}\geq f_{i} for all i≠4i\neq 4. Also note that deg+⁡v2=1\deg^{+}v_{2}=1, and that v2v_{2} receives chips from v4v_{4}. Then f2≥f4f_{2}\geq f_{4}. Since f3≥f2f_{3}\geq f_{2}, we have f3≥f4f_{3}\geq f_{4}.
∎
Let the cc-convergent period Tc​(G)T_{c}(G) of a graph GG be min⁡(T)\min(T) over all parallel chip-firing games on GG with at least cc chips. Note that Tc​(G)=1T_{c}(G)=1 for any undirected graph GG, regardless of cc Bu et al. (2022). We will show that the same is not true for directed graphs.
Theorem 3.10.
For the useful orientation of KnK_{n}, with c=n⁡(n−3)2+1c=\frac{n(n-3)}{2}+1, we have Tc​(Kn)=Ω⁡((n−1)!)T_{c}(K_{n})=\Omega((n-1)!).
Proof.
Since the number of chips on each vertex does not change after a full period, the following equations must hold.
