0&0&0&0&0&\ldots&0&0&1&n-2\end{array}\right)
(3.4)
We next apply the rest of the Gauss-Jordan Elimination algorithm for Mn−2M_{n-2} on MnM_{n} to create a new matrix Mn′M_{n}^{\prime}. The (n−2)(n-2)th and (n−1)(n-1)th columns of Mn′M_{n}^{\prime} will have arbitrary constants, but the rest of Mn′M_{n}^{\prime} will be in reduced row echelon form. In other words, we solve for f2,…,fn−2f_{2},\,\ldots,\,f_{n-2} in terms of fn−1f_{n-1} and fnf_{n}. Recall that Mn′2,n−2=−Rn−22,n−2=−Tn−2M_{n}^{\prime 2,\,n-2}=-R_{n-2}^{2,\,n-2}=-T_{n-2}. To cancel Mn′2,n−2M_{n}^{\prime 2,\,n-2}, we note that Mn′n−2,n−2=1M_{n}^{\prime n-2,\,n-2}=1. Thus, we add Tn−2T_{n-2} times the (n−2)(n-2)th row to the second row, leaving f3f_{3} in terms of only fnf_{n}. If Mnn−2,nM_{n}^{n-2,\,n} were equal to n−3=Mn−1n−2,n−1n-3=M_{n-1}^{n-2,\,n-1}, we would now have that Mn′2,n=Tn−1M_{n}^{\prime 2,\,n}=T_{n-1}. However, Mnn−2,nM_{n}^{n-2,\,n} is instead Mn−1n−2,n−1+1M_{n-1}^{n-2,\,n-1}+1. Therefore, adding Tn−2T_{n-2} times the (n−2)(n-2)th row to the 2nd row adds an additional quantity of Tn−2T_{n-2} to Mn′2,nM_{n}^{\prime 2,\,n}, so that we now have Mn′2,n=Tn−2+Tn−1M_{n}^{\prime 2,\,n}=T_{n-2}+T_{n-1}.
We now proceed with the rest of the Gauss-Jordan Elimination algorithm for Mn−1M_{n-1} on Mn′M_{n}^{\prime} to create a new matrix Mn′′M_{n}^{\prime\prime}, in which only the (n−1)(n-1)th column is not in reduced row echelon form. In other words, we solve for f2,…,fn−1f_{2},\,\ldots,\,f_{n-1} in terms of fnf_{n}. Recall that Mn′′2,n−1=−Tn−1M_{n}^{\prime\prime 2,\,n-1}=-T_{n-1}. To cancel Mn′′2,n−1M_{n}^{\prime\prime 2,\,n-1}, we note that Mn′′n−1,n−1=1M_{n}^{\prime\prime n-1,\,n-1}=1. Thus, we add Tn−1T_{n-1} times the (n−1)(n-1)th row to the second row, putting the second row completely into reduced row echelon form. Note that Mn′′n−1,n=∑i=1n−1Mni,n=deg+v1=n−2M_{n}^{\prime\prime n-1,\,n}=\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}M_{n}^{i,\,n}=\deg^{+}v_{1}=n-2. Thus,
Mn′′2,n=Mn′2,n+Tn−1(n−2)=Tn−2+Tn−1+Tn−1(n−2)=Tn−2+(n−1)Tn−1.M_{n}^{\prime\prime 2,\,n}=M_{n}^{\prime 2,\,n}+T_{n-1}(n-2)=T_{n-2}+T_{n-1}+T_{n-1}(n-2)=T_{n-2}+(n-1)T_{n-1}.
Since the second row is now in reduced row echelon form, we have
f3=Tn−2+(n−1)​Tn−1.f_{3}=T_{n-2}+(n-1)T_{n-1}.
Since gcd⁡(f1,f3)=gcd⁡(1,f3)=1\gcd(f_{1},\,f_{3})=\gcd(1,\,f_{3})=1, we have Tn=f3=Tn−2+(n−1)​Tn−1T_{n}=f_{3}=T_{n-2}+(n-1)T_{n-1}.
For n≤3n\leq 3, Lemma 3.9 does not hold. However, we can manually solve System 3.2 for n=1, 2, 3n=1,\,2,\,3 to find the minimal solution fi=1f_{i}=1 for all ii. Thus, we set T1=T2=T3=1T_{1}=T_{2}=T_{3}=1. For n≥4n\geq 4, we use the above recursive formula. By the OEIS Sequence A058279 Sloan and Inc. (2024), Tn=Θ⁡((n−1)!)T_{n}=\Theta((n-1)!).
Recall that fif_{i} is the number of times that viv_{i} fires in the first TT rounds. Thus, T≥fiT\geq f_{i}. Since f3≥Tnf_{3}\geq T_{n} for n≥4n\geq 4 and f1≥Tnf_{1}\geq T_{n} for n≤3n\leq 3, we have that T≥TnT\geq T_{n} for all parallel chip-firing games in which at least one vertex fires each round. Thus,
Tc​(Kn)≥Tn=Θ⁡((n−1)!)⟹Tc​(Kn)=Ω⁡((n−1)!).T_{c}(K_{n})\geq T_{n}=\Theta((n-1)!)\implies T_{c}(K_{n})=\Omega((n-1)!).
Example 3.11.
Figure 4 shows an example of a parallel chip-firing game on K4K_{4} with T=T4=4T=T_{4}=4. The vector (f1,f2,f3,f4)=(1, 3, 4, 2)(f_{1},\,f_{2},\,f_{3},\,f_{4})=(1,\,3,\,4,\,2) is indeed a solution to System 3.1 for K4K_{4}.
